{"text": " Now let's discuss a special thing for our quicksort. The worst case time complexity of a quicksort is when it comes. Let's look at this lecture only when the time complexity is already clear. We already know about space complexity. Otherwise, we can skip this lecture and look at it after the time complexity chapter. The worst case time complexity comes in a quicksort when my entire array is sorted in ascending order or descending order. And this is when my pivot element is always the smallest element or the largest element. How? Let's assume that my array looks like this. 1, 2, 3, 4, 5. Now what will happen if I take my pivot element as the last element? If my pivot element is always the last element, then my pivot element will always be the largest element of the array. Why? Because the last element sorted in ascending order is always the largest of the array. So if I call a quicksort here, then this partition will be divided in two parts. If partition is divided through 5, then what will happen before this? Before this, I will have 1, 2, 3, and then 4. And in the end, I will have empty. Again, the pivot will be 4. If it is 4, then it will be divided in two parts. In the middle, it will be 4. And here, it will be empty. 1, 2, and 3. It will be empty here. Now again, it will be 3 as the pivot. If it is 3 as the pivot, then it will be 3 in the middle. This will also be divided in two parts. In the first part, I will have 1 and 2. And in the second part, it will be empty. After this, again, the pivot will choose 2 here. If I have two around, then this will come empty. And here, I will have 1. So what happened every time? Every time, my pivot element is the last element. That is, the largest element. So every time, the size of my array. Can I say that if I consider this as a recursion? So what is the size of my array on my first level? How many times do I have to reverse? How many times will my loop run in the partition? Here, I am talking about the partition function. In the partition function, my loop will run N times. At this level, in the partition, my time complexity will come N minus 1. At this level, my time complexity will come N minus 2. At this level, N minus 3 will come. At this level, N minus 4 will come. And in this way, it will go up to 1. So can I say that at one level of the recursion, the time complexity is N. Then there is N minus 1, then there is N minus 2, then there is N minus 3. And in my total time complexity, what do I do? I add them all. So N plus N minus 1, plus N minus 2, plus N minus 3. In this way, if I add 3, plus 2, plus 1, to all of them. Can I say that I have an arithmetic progression here? Can I say it in reverse? 1 plus 2, plus 3, plus 4. And up to where? Up to N. So what is its sum? N plus 1 by 2. What is this? N square plus N by 2. And I can say this that it will be big of N square. So when will be the worst case? The worst case will be when in my ascending order, my pivot element is always the largest element. In that case, every single level will increase the time complexity. From which the final worst-case time complexity will be equal to N square. And in the same case, if it is sorted in descending order, i.e. if this area is in reverse, then it is always the smallest element. So in that case, the same N square time complexity goes. Now the question is that the worst-case time complexity of Quixote is asked in actual interviews. So you should already know this. In fact, it is very important to remember this. Make a note that the worst-case time complexity comes into my Quixote when my pivot element is always the smallest element or always the largest element.", "segments": [{"id": 0, "seek": 0, "start": 0.0, "end": 2.88, "text": " Now let's discuss a special thing for our quicksort.", "tokens": [50364, 823, 718, 311, 2248, 257, 2121, 551, 337, 527, 1702, 82, 477, 13, 50508], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.5018573617035488, "compression_ratio": 1.981981981981982, "no_speec